mrr(1) "v__VERSION__" "modctl __DATE__"

# NAME

mrr - normalize and organize a local music library

# SYNOPSIS

*mrr* <command> [options] [_path_]++
*mrr video* <command> [options] [_path_]++
*mrr playlist* <command> [options] [_path_]++
*mrr sync ipod|sdcard* [options] <_device-path_> [_path_]++
*mrr sync navidrome* <command> [options] [_playlist_]++
*mrr login|logout* [options]

# DESCRIPTION

*mrr* normalizes a local music library into a consistent, sanitized directory
hierarchy derived from internal metadata tags. Files are moved; no metadata
tags are modified by *rename*.

The intended workflow for a full library update is:

	mrr rename    # move files into the normalized structure++
	mrr lyrics    # fetch and embed lyrics from LRCLIB++
	mrr check     # audit for metadata and structural issues++
	mrr sums      # generate md5 checksums

All commands accept an optional _path_ argument and default to the current
directory. Mode is auto-detected from the path: a directory containing audio
files directly is treated as a single album; a directory without audio files
directly inside is treated as a library root and processed recursively; some
commands also accept a path to a single audio file.

Device-target playlist selection, syncing to a device, and Navidrome sync are
a separate, optional workflow; see *PLAYLISTS*, *DEVICE SYNC*, and *NAVIDROME
SYNC* below.

Music videos are managed as a separate command family and directory tree; see
*VIDEO SUPPORT* below.

# DIRECTORY STRUCTURE

Files are organized as:

	_first-letter_/_artist_/_[year] album_/_track title_.ext

The first-letter bucket is a single lowercase character: *a*-*z* for artists
whose name begins with a letter, or *0* for all others (digits, symbols). If
the *DATE* tag is absent the year prefix is omitted entirely. Multi-disc albums
prefix each track with the disc number (*1-01 title.flac*); single-disc albums
do not.

All strings pass through a sanitization pipeline before use: Unicode
transliteration to ASCII, lowercasing, stripping of non-alphanumeric characters,
space normalization, and truncation. A small hardcoded set of overrides handles
known edge cases (e.g. *AC/DC* → *ac⁄dc*).

## Album directory contents

Each album directory may contain the following:

[[ *Path*
:[ *Contents*
|  \*.flac, \*.mp3, \*.m4a
:  Audio files
|  folder.jpg, folder.png
:  Primary artwork (static)
|  folder.webp, folder.mp4
:  Primary artwork (animated)
|  \*.log, \*.cue, \*.m3u, \*.m3u8
:  Text assets
|  sums.md5
:  Checksums (generated by *sums*)
|  artwork/
:  Additional images
|  scans/
:  High-resolution scans
|  extras/
:  All other files

*{target}.m3u8* files (e.g. *ipod.m3u8*) carry special meaning; see
*PLAYLISTS* below.

# COMMANDS

## mrr rename [_path_]

Reads metadata tags and moves files into the normalized hierarchy. Unknown
files (those that don't match any recognized category) are left in place and
reported as warnings. The *extras/* subdirectory acts as a catch-all for known
non-audio assets and opts out of the unknown-file check. Primary artwork named
*folder.jpeg* is renamed to *folder.jpg*.

Collision detection runs before any files are moved. If two source files
resolve to the same destination path, or if any destination path already exists
on the filesystem, the entire run is aborted and all conflicts are reported.

*--dry-run*
	Preview all planned moves without touching the filesystem.

## mrr lyrics [_path_]

Fetches lyrics from LRCLIB (https://lrclib.net) and embeds them into audio
tags. For each track, LRCLIB is queried by title, artist, album, and duration.
The search progressively relaxes the duration constraint (±1 s, ±2 s) before
falling back to a fuzzy search; tracks with no result are skipped and reported
in the summary.

Synced (LRC) lyrics are embedded in FLAC files only. Unsynced lyrics are
embedded in MP3 and M4A via the normalized *LYRICS* tag. If only synced lyrics
are available from LRCLIB for an MP3 or M4A track, that track is skipped.

Existing lyrics tags are never overwritten unless *--force* is passed.

*--force*
	Re-fetch and overwrite existing lyrics tags.

## mrr check [_path_]

Audits a library or album for metadata and structural issues. Emits all
findings to stdout grouped by album and exits non-zero if any are present.

Checks include:

- Missing *TITLE*, *TRACKNUMBER*, *DATE*, or artist tags
- Inconsistent *ALBUMARTIST* or *ALBUM* across tracks
- Partial *DISCNUMBER* coverage or duplicate track numbers
- Missing *REPLAYGAIN_TRACK_GAIN* or *REPLAYGAIN_ALBUM_GAIN* tags
- Embedded artwork inside audio files
- Missing or duplicate primary artwork (*folder.jpg* / *folder.png* /
  *folder.webp* / *folder.mp4*). One static image plus one animated file is
  allowed as a fallback pair.
- Missing *sums.md5*
- Playlist manifest issues (*{target}.m3u8*): unrecognized target name, a
  stale entry with no matching track, or a duplicate entry
- Path conformance: whether *rename* would move any file (library mode only)

Read-only; makes no filesystem changes.

## mrr sums [_path_]

Computes MD5 checksums for all files in an album and writes a *sums.md5* file
formatted for use with *md5sum -c*. Binary files (audio, images) use an
asterisk prefix; text files (.log, .cue, .m3u, .m3u8) use a two-space prefix.
*sums.md5* itself is never included. Filenames are listed in sorted order for
stable, diffable output.

In library mode, albums that already have a *sums.md5* are silently skipped.
In single-album mode, an existing *sums.md5* is an error unless *--force* is
passed.

*--force*
	Regenerate *sums.md5* even if one already exists.

## mrr inspect _file_

Displays the raw tag values for a single audio file alongside the sanitized
forms that *rename* would use for path construction. Accepts *.flac*, *.mp3*,
and *.m4a* files only. Read-only; makes no filesystem changes.

## mrr login [options]

Stores Navidrome server credentials, used by *sync navidrome* commands (see
*NAVIDROME SYNC* below). *--url* and *--username* are prompted for
interactively if omitted; the password is never accepted as a flag — it's
prompted for (masked) by default, or read from stdin with *--password-stdin*
for scripting, e.g. piping from a password manager:

	pass show navidrome | mrr login --url https://nav.example.com \
		--username mario --password-stdin

Credentials are validated against the server before anything is saved.
Stored at *$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/musicrename/navidrome.json*, mode *0600*. Only one
server can be configured at a time; running *login* again replaces it.

*--url* _url_
	Navidrome server URL.
*--username* _name_
	Navidrome username.
*--password-stdin*
	Read the password from stdin instead of prompting.

## mrr logout

Clears stored Navidrome credentials. Not an error if not currently logged
in.

## mrr playlist select <_target_> [_album-path_]

Interactively selects which tracks from an album are included for a device
sync target (currently *ipod* or *sdcard*), writing the selection to
*{target}.m3u8* in the album directory. If every track ends up unselected,
the manifest file is deleted rather than left behind empty.

If _album-path_ is omitted it defaults to the current directory.

*--skip-md5*
	Do not update *sums.md5* even if one already exists in the album.

## mrr playlist check [_library-root-root_]

Audits the library-wide *playlists/* tree (see *PLAYLISTS* below) for
broken entries, unrecognized names in a *#TARGETS:* directive, duplicate
*#NAVIDROME-ID* values across files, a directive (*#PLAYLIST:*,
*#NAVIDROME-ID:*, *#TARGETS:*, or *#SORT:*) repeated within a single file,
those same directives appearing in a different relative order than
musicrename itself would write them in, and a missing or stale
*playlists/sums.md5* (files present with no recorded entry, or entries
with no matching file). Read-only; exits non-zero if any findings are
present.

Findings for album-local *{target}.m3u8* manifests (the ones *playlist
select* writes) are reported by *mrr check* instead, not here.

If _library-root-root_ is omitted it defaults to the current directory.

## mrr playlist rename [_library-root-root_]

Scans the same *playlists/* tree as *playlist check* and renames each file
to a filesystem-safe name derived from its *#PLAYLIST:* directive. If a
*playlists/sums.md5* already exists, the renamed file's entry is relabeled
to its new name.

A file with no *#PLAYLIST:* directive, or one whose name sanitizes to an
empty string, is skipped and reported rather than treated as an error. A
file already at its correctly-sanitized name produces no output at all.

If _library-root-root_ is omitted it defaults to the current directory.

*--dry-run*
	Preview all planned renames without touching the filesystem.

## mrr playlist sums [_library-root-root_]

Computes MD5 checksums for every file under the library-wide *playlists/*
tree (see *PLAYLISTS* below) recursively, and writes them to a single
*playlists/sums.md5* covering the whole tree. Unlike album or video
*sums.md5*, which are one per directory, there is no library-wide-vs-
single-item distinction to make here.

If _library-root-root_ is omitted it defaults to the current directory.

*--force*
	Overwrite an existing *playlists/sums.md5*.

## mrr playlist create <_name_> [_library-root-root_]

Creates a new file under the library-wide *playlists/* tree with a
*#PLAYLIST:* directive set to _name_ (and a *#TARGETS:* directive, if
*--targets* is given) and no entries.

An existing file at that destination is an error; this command never overwrites
an existing file. If a *playlists/sums.md5* already exists, the new file's
entry is added to it.

If _library-root-root_ is omitted it defaults to the current directory.

*--targets* _t1,t2_
	Comma-separated target names; omit the flag entirely for "applies to
	every target".

## mrr playlist targets <_playlist_> [*--set* _t1,t2_ | *--clear*]

Rewrites _playlist_'s *#TARGETS:* directive: *--set* replaces it with the
given comma-separated target list (an empty value is valid and means
"applies to no target" which is distinct from omitting the flag); *--clear*
removes the directive entirely, meaning the playlist applies to every
target again. Exactly one of *--set*/*--clear* is required.

_playlist_ must already exist; this command will never create a new file (use
*playlist create* for that).

## mrr playlist entries add <_playlist_> [_path_]...

Appends each given _path_, in order, to _playlist_'s entry list, then
rewrites the file. Each _path_ may be relative to the current working
directory or absolute; either way it's resolved and stored relative to the
library root.

A _path_ that doesn't resolve to a real file under the library root is
skipped and reported.

With no _path_ arguments, opens an interactive directory browser instead:
arrow keys (or *j*/*k*) move the cursor, *left*/*h*/*backspace* goes up a
level, *right*/*l*/*enter* descends into a directory or, if is an album,
opens a checkbox picker for its tracks. Press */* to filter the current
directory's listing by substring.

Nothing is written until the browser is left: *esc*/*q* at the top level saves
everything staged across the whole session; *ctrl+c* discards all changes.

_playlist_ must already exist (use *playlist create* to scaffold a new one
first).

## mrr playlist entries remove <_playlist_> [*--artist* _name_] [*--album* _name_] [*--dry-run*]

With neither *--artist* nor *--album* given, shows every current entry as a
pre-checked checkbox (like *playlist select*); uncheck the ones to remove,
then confirm. With either flag given, matches non-interactively instead:
every entry whose resolved track's tags match all the given flags
(case-insensitive) is removed.

Reading tags is scoped to this playlist's entries only. An entry that doesn't
resolve to a real file, or whose tags can't be read, is shown but never
auto-matched by *--artist*/*--album* (since there's nothing to match against);
it stays unless explicitly unchecked in interactive mode.

_playlist_ must already exist.

*--artist* _name_
	Remove entries whose ARTIST tag matches _name_ (case-insensitive).
*--album* _name_
	Remove entries whose ALBUM tag matches _name_ (case-insensitive). Combined
	with *--artist*, both must match.
*--dry-run*
	Preview what would be removed without changing the file.

## mrr playlist entries reorder <_playlist_>

Opens an interactive full-screen editor listing _playlist_'s current
entries. Move the cursor with the arrow keys (or *j*/*k*, *Home*/*End*,
*PgUp*/*PgDn*); press *space* to "grab" the entry under the cursor, then
the same movement keys move it instead of the cursor, shifting everything
between the old and new position by one to make room; press *space* again
to release it. Press *enter* to save the new order, or
*esc*/*ctrl+c*/*q* to cancel without changing anything.

Filenames are shown immediately; track metadata loads in the background
afterward, filling in as each file is read but you can reorder freely while
it's still loading.

_playlist_ must already exist.

## mrr playlist sort <_playlist_> [_fields_] [*--shuffle*] [*--dry-run*]

Reorders _playlist_'s entries by _fields_, a comma-separated list of track
metadata fields in precedence order (the first breaks the most ties):
*artist*, *albumartist*, *album*, *year*, *disc*, *track*, *title*. An
entry with no value for the field being compared sorts after any entry
that has one; an entry that doesn't resolve to a real file at all sorts
after every resolvable one. Ties left unbroken by every given field keep
their current relative order.

With neither _fields_ nor *--shuffle* given, reapplies whichever criteria
were last used on this playlist (remembered in a *#SORT:* directive
written back on every successful sort or shuffle) but this errors if
nothing has been remembered yet and none is given now. Explicit _fields_
or *--shuffle* always supersede and become the new remembered criteria.

By default duplicate entries are removed when sorting. Pass *--skip-dedupe* to
turn this off.

_playlist_ must already exist.

*--shuffle*
	Randomize entry order instead of sorting by fields.
*--dry-run*
	Preview the new order without changing the file or its remembered
	criteria.
*--skip-dedupe*
	Do not remove duplicate entries before sorting.

## mrr playlist entries dedupe <_playlist_> [*--check*] [*--dry-run*]

Removes duplicate entries from _playlist_, keeping each one's first
occurrence; every other entry's relative order is left completely
untouched.

*--check* and *--dry-run* are mutually exclusive: *--check* exits non-zero if
any duplicates are found; *--dry-run* previews what would be removed.

_playlist_ must already exist.

*--check*
	Report whether duplicates exist.
*--dry-run*
	Preview what would be removed without changing the file.

## mrr sync ipod <_device-path_> [_path_]

Syncs the library to an iPod running Rockbox (https://www.rockbox.org)
mounted at _device-path_ (e.g. */media/you/IPOD*). Only tracks selected
with *mrr playlist select ipod* are copied; see *DEVICE SYNC* below for
the full workflow.

FLAC, MP3, and M4A tracks are copied through unchanged; nothing is
transcoded for this target. Album artwork is resized to fit within 400
pixels and shipped as a separate file alongside the tracks.

Compares the library against whatever is already on the device, checks
that there's enough free space for the changes, then asks for
confirmation before adding, updating, or removing anything on the
device (removals only ever affect the device copy, your library files
are never touched).

If _path_ is omitted it defaults to the current directory.

*--dry-run*
	Preview what would happen without changing anything.
*--yes*
	Skip the confirmation prompt.
*--verbose*
	List every file added, updated, or removed instead of just totals.

## mrr sync sdcard <_device-path_> [_path_]

Syncs the library to an SD card (e.g. for a car head unit) mounted at
_device-path_. Only tracks selected with *mrr playlist select sdcard*
are copied; see *DEVICE SYNC* below for the full workflow.

Only MP3 is supported on this target; anything else (FLAC, M4A) is
transcoded automatically. Album artwork is resized to fit within 500
pixels and embedded directly into each track rather than shipped as a
separate file (changing an album's artwork alone re-embeds it into
every already-synced track for that album, even if none of the audio
itself changed).

Otherwise behaves exactly like *mrr sync ipod*: compares the library
against the device, checks free space, and asks for confirmation
before changing anything.

If _path_ is omitted it defaults to the current directory.

*--dry-run*
	Preview what would happen without changing anything.
*--yes*
	Skip the confirmation prompt.
*--verbose*
	List every file added, updated, or removed instead of just totals.

## mrr sync navidrome pull [_playlist_]

Pulls playlists from the configured Navidrome server (*mrr login*) into the
*playlists/* tree, overwriting local content with whatever the server
currently holds. See *NAVIDROME SYNC* below for the intended workflow. If a
*playlists/sums.md5* already exists, every local write (create or update)
and delete keeps that one entry current.

With no argument, pulls every playlist. With _playlist_ given (a path to an
existing local playlist file), pulls just that one; if the server confirms
it no longer exists there, the local file is deleted to match.

*--dry-run*
	Print what would happen without changing any files.
*--skip-scan*
	Skip the library scan normally triggered before pulling.

## mrr sync navidrome push [_playlist_]

Pushes playlists from the *playlists/* tree to the configured Navidrome
server, overwriting remote content with whatever the local file currently
says. A local playlist with no *#NAVIDROME-ID* yet is created on the
server, and the assigned ID is written back into the local file.

With no argument, pushes every playlist under *playlists/*. With _playlist_
given, pushes just that one.

*--dry-run*
	Print what would happen without changing anything, locally or remotely.
*--skip-scan*
	Skip the library scan normally triggered before pushing.

## mrr sync navidrome delete <_playlist_>

Permanently deletes one playlist: removes it from the Navidrome server,
then removes the local file (and, if a *playlists/sums.md5* already
exists, that file's entry in it). _playlist_ must already have a
*#NAVIDROME-ID* (i.e. it's been pushed at least once); this command errors
immediately, without deleting anything, otherwise. Prompts for confirmation
unless *--yes* is passed (this cannot be undone).

*--yes*
	Skip the confirmation prompt.

# METADATA

*Album artist*
	Uses the *ALBUMARTIST* tag. Falls back to the *ARTIST* tag of the track
	with the lowest *TRACKNUMBER* on the album if *ALBUMARTIST* is absent.

*Year*
	Extracted from the *DATE* tag. Full ISO-8601 dates and year-month values
	are accepted; only the four-character year component is used.

*Disc numbers*
	All-or-nothing per album. If any track has a *DISCNUMBER* tag, every
	track must have one; otherwise the album is skipped with an error.

*Track numbers*
	Zero-padded to two digits. Switches to three digits for the whole album if
	any track number exceeds 99. A value of *0* is valid and represents a
	pre-gap or hidden track.

*Missing tags*
	A missing *YEAR* omits the year prefix from the album folder. A missing
	*TITLE* falls back to the original filename stem. A missing *TRACKNUMBER*
	sorts the file alphabetically among its untracked peers. Missing both
	*ARTIST* and *ALBUMARTIST* is a fatal error for that album.

# PLAYLISTS

Two distinct kinds of playlist exist.

*Album-local target manifests* are *{target}.m3u8* files (e.g. *ipod.m3u8*,
*sdcard.m3u8*) living inside an album directory, listing which tracks from
that album are selected for a given device sync target. Managed with *mrr
playlist select*. Entry order carries no meaning.

*Library-wide playlists* live under a top-level *playlists/* directory which
is a sibling of the library root(s), and can span albums and library roots.
Entry order is meaningful (playback order). Each file may carry these directive
lines at its top:

[[ *Directive*
:[ *Meaning*
|  #PLAYLIST:_name_
:  Display name, independent of the (sanitized) filename
|  #NAVIDROME-ID:_id_
:  Correlated Navidrome playlist ID; absent if never pushed
|  #TARGETS:_target_,_target_,...
:  Which sync targets this applies to; absent means every target

Entries below the directives are plain paths, one per line, relative to the
*playlists/* directory's parent (the directory that contains the library
root(s)).

Use *mrr playlist check* to audit this tree. Subdirectories under
*playlists/* carry no special meaning and are fine to use for your own
organization.

# DEVICE SYNC

*mrr* can copy a curated subset of the library onto removable storage
mounted directly on this machine: an iPod running Rockbox
(https://www.rockbox.org), or a generic MP3-only SD card for something
like a car head unit.

The workflow is select, then sync:

	mrr playlist select ipod        # choose which tracks to include, per album++
	mrr sync ipod /media/you/IPOD   # copy them to the device

Only tracks explicitly selected for a target (see *PLAYLISTS* above) are
ever copied to it. Each sync compares the library against whatever the
device already has, adds anything missing, updates anything that's
changed, and removes anything no longer selected (removals only ever
affect the device copy, never your library). Before making any changes,
*mrr* checks that the device has enough free space and asks for
confirmation; pass *--yes* to skip the prompt, *--dry-run* to preview
without changing anything, or *--verbose* to list every file instead of
just totals.

*ipod* and *sdcard* differ only in what happens to the files themselves:
*ipod* copies FLAC/MP3/M4A through unchanged and ships artwork as a
separate file; *sdcard* transcodes anything that isn't already MP3 and
embeds artwork directly into each track, since car head units commonly
can't play FLAC and may not show an external cover file at all. See *mrr
sync ipod* and *mrr sync sdcard* above for details.

# NAVIDROME SYNC

*mrr* can sync the library-wide *playlists/* tree with a Navidrome
(https://navidrome.org) server over its Subsonic-compatible API. Run *mrr
login* once to store credentials, then use *mrr sync navidrome
pull*|*push*|*delete*.

The intended workflow is pull, edit, push, run as one session:

	mrr sync navidrome pull    # bring playlists/ up to date from the server++
	                            # ... make local edits ...++
	mrr sync navidrome push    # send those edits back

Both directions overwrite the side being synced *to*, completely: pull
replaces local file content with the server's, push replaces the server's
content with the local file's. Editing a playlist on the server (e.g. from
a phone) *during* an open pull-edit-push session gets silently overwritten
by the eventual push: treat this as a checkout/check-in, not a continuous
merge.

A *#TARGETS:* or *#SORT:* directive is synced through the Navidrome
playlist's comment field, appended as a *[musicrename:sort=...;targets=...]*
suffix (either or both keys present, as applicable) so a normal,
human-written description can still live in the same field.

Deleting a playlist outside of *mrr sync navidrome delete* is handled
gracefully on the next pull: a playlist removed on the server disappears
locally too, while a playlist file removed locally by hand (e.g. plain
*rm*) simply reappears on the next pull instead of being treated as
intentional. *mrr sync navidrome delete* is the only way to perform a real,
permanent deletion.

# VIDEO SUPPORT

Music videos (one video per track) are managed as a tree completely separate
from the audio library, with its own root path and *video* command family.
Videos are typically sourced via *yt-dlp*(1) and carry no reliable embedded
metadata, so (unlike audio tracks) a video's Artist/Title are never read
from the file itself. Instead they live in a *musicvideo.nfo* sidecar that
*mrr* owns and writes; it is not intended to be hand-edited.

## Video directory structure

	_first-letter_/_artist_/_title_/_title_.ext++
		musicvideo.nfo++
		info.txt

Bucketing and sanitization reuse the same first-letter logic and manual
override table as the audio library. Title doubles as both the directory name
and the file basename since a video has no track number, disc number, or year
in its path. Recognized video extensions are *.mp4*, *.webm*, and *.mkv*.

## Video directory contents

[[ *Path*
:[ *Contents*
|  \*.mp4, \*.webm, \*.mkv
:  Video file
|  musicvideo.nfo
:  Metadata sidecar (see below)
|  info.txt
:  Source URL/description, written once by *fetch*; user-owned afterward
|  sums.md5
:  Checksums (generated by *video sums*)

## musicvideo.nfo

A minimal, Kodi/Jellyfin-compatible XML sidecar:

```
<musicvideo>
  <title>Crazy in Love</title>
  <artist>Beyoncé</artist>
  <album>Dangerously in Love</album>
  <year>2003</year>
</musicvideo>
```

*title* and *artist* are required. *album* and *year* are optional,
informational only. The nfo is always machine-written via *video add* or *video
edit*; hand-editing is unsupported and unnecessary.

## mrr video fetch _url_ [_destination_]

Downloads a video via *yt-dlp*(1) and writes a generated *info.txt*
(url/title/uploader/uploaded/description). The URL is cleaned to its bare
video ID first — playlist, timestamp, and tracking query parameters are
discarded — supporting *youtube.com/watch*, */shorts/*, */embed/*, and
*youtu.be* short links. Standalone download step: it does not prompt for
artist/title or file the video into the library. Requires *yt-dlp* on *PATH*.

If _destination_ is omitted it defaults to the current working directory.

## mrr video add _file_ [_video-root_]

Sanitizes artist/title, computes the destination under _video-root_, moves
the video file into place, and writes *musicvideo.nfo*. An *info.txt* sitting
alongside the source video (as written by *fetch*) is carried along too, if
present.

*--artist* _name_
	Artist name. Prompted for interactively if omitted.
*--title* _title_
	Video title. Prompted for interactively if omitted.
*--album* _album_
	Album name (optional).
*--year* _year_
	Release year (optional).

Errors if the destination directory already exists; there is no *--force*
overwrite path. If _video-root_ is omitted it defaults to the current working
directory.

## mrr video edit [_file-or-directory_]

Creates or updates a video's *musicvideo.nfo*. The argument can be the video
file itself or its directory; if omitted it defaults to the current
directory, so running this from inside a video's folder needs no
argument at all. A missing nfo is not an error and one is created fresh if
necessary (requires a recognized video file already present in the target
directory).

Accepts the same *--artist*/*--title*/*--album*/*--year* flags as *add*. Any
field not passed as a flag is prompted for interactively, pre-filled with its
current value. To clear an already-set optional field non-interactively, pass
it as an empty value, e.g. *--year ""*.

Only ever rewrites the nfo; run *video rename* afterward if artist or title
changed.

## mrr video rename [_video-root_]

Reconciles video locations with their *musicvideo.nfo* metadata: moves any
video (with its nfo and *info.txt*, if present) whose current path no
longer matches its Artist/Title. A video directory with no *musicvideo.nfo*,
or with more than one video file, is skipped with a warning.

*--dry-run*
	Preview all planned moves without touching the filesystem.

## mrr video sums [_path_]

Computes MD5 checksums for a video's directory in the same *sums.md5* format
as audio *sums*. Auto-detects single-video vs. video-root mode the same way
audio *sums* does.

*--force*
	Overwrite existing *sums.md5* files.

## mrr video check [_path_]

Audits the video library for metadata and structural issues: a directory
missing exactly one video file, a missing or incomplete *musicvideo.nfo*,
path conformance (video-root mode only), and a missing *sums.md5*. Read-only;
exits non-zero if any findings are present.

## mrr video inspect _file_

Displays a video's raw and sanitized Title/Artist from *musicvideo.nfo*,
alongside Album/Year as stored. Read-only.

## mrr video extract-audio _file_

Remuxes (not re-encodes) a video's audio stream into a standalone file next to
it, tags it from *musicvideo.nfo*, and computes ReplayGain via *rsgain*.
Requires an existing *musicvideo.nfo* with both artist and title set.

By default, errors if a derived audio file already exists.

*--retag*
	Update only the derived audio file's tags (doesn't recompute
	ReplayGain). For when *video edit* changed artist/title but the video's
	actual content is unchanged.
*--force*
	Fully re-extract even if a derived audio file already exists. For when
	the source video's actual content changed (e.g., a re-fetch).

*--retag* and *--force* are mutually exclusive. More than one existing
derived audio file for the video is an error, even with *--force* so you
need resolve it manually first.

